From: xPDFSDK team <noreply@xpdfsdk.local>
Date: 2026-05-17
Subject: [PATCH] Add Android ATrace markers inside AGG renderer + JPEG decoder

Background
==========
On image-heavy PDFs (picture books, magazines), single-page renders can
take 7+ seconds even after the JPEG downscale-on-decode patch (0003).
Perfetto traces of `xpdf.renderBitmap.region[N]` show the time is 100%
spent inside `FPDF_RenderPageBitmap` with the session thread fully
CPU-bound. The Kotlin-side trace markers cannot tell us *what inside
PDFium* consumes the time -- we need markers inside PDFium itself.

This patch (0004 -- applies on top of 0003)
===========================================
Adds `ATrace_beginSection / ATrace_endSection` markers around the hot
code paths inside the AGG rasterizer. Each marker appears as a slice
named `pdfium.*` in any Perfetto / systrace capture, nested under the
existing `xpdf.renderBitmap.*` slice from the Kotlin layer. The slice
widths directly reveal which inner stage dominates wall time.

Markers added
=============
  fpdfsdk/fpdf_view.cpp
    - pdfium.FPDF_RenderPageBitmap   (outermost entry point)

  core/fpdfapi/render/cpdf_renderstatus.cpp
    - pdfium.RenderObjectList        (per-form page-object walk)
    - pdfium.ProcessText             (text-run draw)
    - pdfium.ProcessPath             (vector path draw)
    - pdfium.ProcessImage            (image draw)
    - pdfium.ProcessShading          (shading patterns)
    - pdfium.ProcessForm             (nested XObject form)
    - pdfium.DrawObjWithBackground   (fallback object draw)
    - pdfium.ProcessTransparency     (SMask / alpha-group setup)
    - pdfium.LoadSMask               (soft-mask image decode)

  core/fpdfapi/page/cpdf_pageimagecache.cpp
    - pdfium.StartGetCachedBitmap    (image cache lookup; miss = decode)

  core/fxcodec/jpeg/jpegmodule.cpp
    - pdfium.JpegRewind              (libjpeg jpeg_start_decompress +
                                      scanline IDCT pass; this is the
                                      core decode cost)

Implementation notes
====================
1. All markers are gated `#if defined(__ANDROID__)`. On non-Android
   builds (desktop PDFium, Chromium, ChromeOS) they compile to nothing.
2. The `<android/trace.h>` header is part of the NDK sysroot at API 23+
   and matches our minSdk -- no extra dependency.
3. A small RAII helper `XpdfScopedTrace` is added in each .cpp where
   needed so functions with many early returns (e.g. ProcessTransparency,
   LoadSMask) get a single begin/end pair regardless of which branch
   returns.
4. The BUILD.gn change adds `libs += [ "android" ]` to the `pdfium`
   component on Android, so the NDK's libandroid.so is linked at build
   time (it contains the ATrace_* symbols).

Cost
====
- When tracing disabled (kernel trace gate off): `ATrace_beginSection`
  is a single bitmask check + early return. Sub-microsecond per call.
- When tracing enabled: ~1-5 µs per begin/end pair.

Net cost on a 7-second render with hundreds of objects: well under 10ms
overhead when tracing is on. Effectively free when off.

Usage
=====
After building with this patch applied:

  adb shell setprop debug.xpdf.trace 1
  adb shell am force-stop com.xapper.pdf.reader
  bash android/meta/profile/capture_perfetto_render.sh 15
  # Open picture-book PDF and scroll while it records.
  # Drag the resulting .perfetto-trace into https://ui.perfetto.dev

The `pdfium.*` slices appear as children of the existing `xpdf.*`
slices on the xpdf-session thread. Hover or click to see exact ns
durations.

